1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,086
Every half hour, Saturn's rings lose enough ice to fill

2
00:00:03,086 --> 00:00:04,320
an Olympic swimming pool.

3
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:06,481
It's raining into the planet right now.

4
00:00:06,481 --> 00:00:08,950
So how long do the rings have left?

5
00:00:08,950 --> 00:00:12,500
Stay with me, and you'll see where the ice goes,

6
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:16,200
what the rings looked like when they were new,

7
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,400
and Saturn with nothing around it at all.

8
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:22,573
Look along the sheet.

9
00:00:22,573 --> 00:00:24,107
It is not a solid disk.

10
00:00:24,107 --> 00:00:25,130
It is a crowd.

11
00:00:25,130 --> 00:00:30,290
Chunks of ice, from grains to boulders, each on its own path,

12
00:00:30,290 --> 00:00:35,580
packed so tightly that they read as one bright blade.

13
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:41,100
The blade is enormous sideways — tens of thousands of kilometres —

14
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:42,706
and absurdly thin the other way.

15
00:00:42,706 --> 00:00:45,650
In places the main rings are only about ten metres thick.

16
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:50,770
A playing field stood on edge, stretched around a world.

17
00:00:50,770 --> 00:00:56,150
Why does something that wide look so finished, and so fragile?

18
00:00:56,150 --> 00:01:01,150
Because you are seeing sunlight on ice, not a wall.

19
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:06,150
Photons bounce off clean water ice and come back almost white.

20
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:11,150
A little dust stains the older lanes a warmer colour.

21
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:16,150
The mass under that shine is small for the area it covers.

22
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:19,091
Cassini weighed the rings on its last orbits and found

23
00:01:19,091 --> 00:01:21,150
less than many maps had assumed —

24
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:26,150
on the order of two fifths of the little moon Mimas.

25
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:27,203
A lot of scenery.

26
00:01:27,203 --> 00:01:28,518
Not a lot of stuff.

27
00:01:28,518 --> 00:01:31,150
The mystery starts as a fall, not as a monument.

28
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:37,150
But the picture that brought you here is already the danger.

29
00:01:37,150 --> 00:01:38,304
Does the fall ever stop?

30
00:01:38,304 --> 00:01:40,150
It does not stop while you are watching.

31
00:01:40,150 --> 00:01:46,150
Gravity keeps every chunk moving. The smallest grains are already leaking inward.

32
00:01:46,150 --> 00:01:48,842
What would you see if you waited?

33
00:01:48,842 --> 00:01:51,150
Not a collapse in the news.

34
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:56,150
A slow thinning. Then a planet that has spent the jewellery.

35
00:01:56,150 --> 00:02:03,996
How long that takes is the question we are saving

36
00:02:03,996 --> 00:02:06,350
for the rain.

37
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,140
Now the question changes. What is that jewellery made of?

38
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:19,840
Come in close enough that the blade breaks into pieces.

39
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:24,840
Ice. Not rock. Water ice, the same bright mineral as a comet,

40
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:26,899
dirty in places and clean in others.

41
00:02:26,899 --> 00:02:29,840
Most of the mass sits in chunks you could hold,

42
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:34,840
or in boulders the size of a house.

43
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:38,686
Rocky dust rides with the ice and slowly stains it

44
00:02:38,686 --> 00:02:39,840
cream to tan.

45
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,896
Why call it a ring if it is only a

46
00:02:42,896 --> 00:02:43,812
crowd of snowballs?

47
00:02:43,812 --> 00:02:45,340
Because the crowd is flat.

48
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:49,007
Saturn's gravity, and the moons that shepherd the edges, pack

49
00:02:49,007 --> 00:02:50,840
the paths into a plane.

50
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:55,840
The gaps are emptier roads. The ice that remains still circles.

51
00:02:55,840 --> 00:03:00,840
It does not sit. There is no roof.

52
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:06,340
Drop through those ten metres and you are in open space again.

53
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:11,340
The atmosphere sits far below the inner edge, banded and pale.

54
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:16,340
The unknown is not the recipe. The unknown is the leak.

55
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:20,186
However bright the ice looks, brightness is not a promise

56
00:03:20,186 --> 00:03:21,340
that it stays.

57
00:03:21,340 --> 00:03:26,840
That is the first answer: the famous sheet is only ice,

58
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:32,340
and it is already slipping away.

59
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:34,132
We make one of these every week.

60
00:03:34,132 --> 00:03:36,180
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

61
00:03:41,030 --> 00:03:45,338
Here is one way they go — the smallest grains

62
00:03:45,338 --> 00:03:46,630
get a charge.

63
00:03:46,630 --> 00:03:49,519
Sunlight and the plasma around Saturn knock electrons about, and

64
00:03:49,519 --> 00:03:51,830
a tiny piece of ice stops being neutral.

65
00:03:51,830 --> 00:03:54,687
Saturn's magnetic field already threads the ring plane.

66
00:03:54,687 --> 00:03:56,830
A charged grain feels that field.

67
00:03:56,830 --> 00:03:59,608
It is lifted off its calm circle and guided down,

68
00:03:59,608 --> 00:04:01,830
along the magnetic path, into the upper atmosphere.

69
00:04:01,830 --> 00:04:06,830
There the ice becomes vapour. Water, arriving from above. Ring rain.

70
00:04:06,830 --> 00:04:10,401
We did not need a spacecraft inside that rain to

71
00:04:10,401 --> 00:04:11,830
know it was there.

72
00:04:11,830 --> 00:04:14,771
Astronomers on Earth, using the Keck telescope, watched the glow

73
00:04:14,771 --> 00:04:16,830
of that water in Saturn's upper air

74
00:04:16,830 --> 00:04:21,830
and measured how fast the ice was leaving.

75
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:25,497
At that magnetic rain rate alone, the sheet would last

76
00:04:25,497 --> 00:04:27,330
about three hundred million years.

77
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:32,330
But that is only one path down. Cassini went closer.

78
00:04:32,330 --> 00:04:37,330
In 2017 the orbiter flew twenty-two times through the gap

79
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:42,330
between the innermost ring and the cloud tops.

80
00:04:42,330 --> 00:04:47,330
It was not sampling the magnetic rain from mid-latitudes.

81
00:04:47,330 --> 00:04:51,561
It was flying through something bigger: material pouring off the

82
00:04:51,561 --> 00:04:52,830
innermost ring —

83
00:04:52,830 --> 00:04:57,830
the D ring — straight into Saturn's equator.

84
00:04:57,830 --> 00:05:02,830
That equatorial inflow is a heavier leak than the magnetic rain alone.

85
00:05:02,830 --> 00:05:06,065
Add it to the books, and the sheet's remaining life

86
00:05:06,065 --> 00:05:08,330
drops to around a hundred million years.

87
00:05:08,330 --> 00:05:13,830
So how long does the bright sheet have?

88
00:05:13,830 --> 00:05:19,330
Put both measurements together and the honest answer is a range,

89
00:05:19,330 --> 00:05:24,830
somewhere between about a hundred and three hundred million years.

90
00:05:24,830 --> 00:05:29,830
A blink next to Saturn's four and a half billion years.

91
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:34,830
You would not see either leak as weather from a window.

92
00:05:34,830 --> 00:05:39,830
The grains are too fine, and the fall is too spread out.

93
00:05:39,830 --> 00:05:44,830
What you would see, if you could watch for an age,

94
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:50,850
is the sheet growing thinner, while Saturn's bands stay put.

95
00:05:50,850 --> 00:05:54,530
Now look backward. The rain implies a beginning.

96
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:03,180
Wind the clock back along that same scale,

97
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:07,480
and the sheet changes. More mass. Cleaner ice. A harder white,

98
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:12,180
because the dust had not had time to dirty it.

99
00:06:12,180 --> 00:06:17,180
Saturn would still have been the ringed one — only more so.

100
00:06:17,180 --> 00:06:22,180
A broader blade. A brighter one.

101
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:24,680
The planet underneath was already the planet.

102
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:27,180
The rings look like a late arrival.

103
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:30,751
Why "new", when Saturn itself is about four and a

104
00:06:30,751 --> 00:06:32,180
half billion years old?

105
00:06:32,180 --> 00:06:34,958
Because a heavy, ancient ring would still be heavy, unless

106
00:06:34,958 --> 00:06:37,180
most of its mass has already gone in.

107
00:06:37,180 --> 00:06:40,305
Most researchers read Cassini's low mass as young — a

108
00:06:40,305 --> 00:06:42,180
few hundred million years at most.

109
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:45,513
A few argue the rings could still be old, and

110
00:06:45,513 --> 00:06:47,180
hide their age another way.

111
00:06:47,180 --> 00:06:52,180
The debate is open. The honest line is narrower than a slogan.

112
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:55,305
The rings you see are ice, they are already leaving,

113
00:06:55,305 --> 00:06:57,180
and their birthday is not settled.

114
00:06:57,180 --> 00:07:02,180
What if a moon came too close, and came apart?

115
00:07:02,180 --> 00:07:07,180
That idea fits a young mass, and it is still an idea.

116
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:12,180
Inside the Roche limit, Saturn's gravity can pull a moon into rubble,

117
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:17,180
and rubble in a plane becomes a ring.

118
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:21,510
Whatever their birthday, the rings you know are ice,

119
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:30,960
Take the ice away, and stay with the planet.

120
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:35,660
Saturn is still there. Pale gold bands.

121
00:07:35,660 --> 00:07:40,660
A fast day, a little over ten hours,

122
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:45,660
written as soft stripes in a deep atmosphere.

123
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:50,660
The curve of the planet runs clean.

124
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:55,660
The shadow the rings used to cast on the clouds is gone.

125
00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:57,848
The famous thing was never the body.

126
00:07:57,848 --> 00:08:00,660
It was the ice in orbit around the body.

127
00:08:00,660 --> 00:08:05,660
What would you see, standing in that later sky? A giant,

128
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:09,506
banded and bright, ordinary once you stop using the rings

129
00:08:09,506 --> 00:08:10,660
as the definition.

130
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:13,737
You would feel the oddness in the absence.

131
00:08:13,737 --> 00:08:15,660
The planet does not notice.

132
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:18,993
The rain simply finishes, and the paths that carried it

133
00:08:18,993 --> 00:08:20,660
have nothing left to carry.

134
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:23,013
So the whole story fits in one breath.

135
00:08:23,013 --> 00:08:25,660
You would see a thin sheet of water ice,

136
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:30,660
vast and bright, already raining into Saturn.

137
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:33,895
Magnetic rain measured from Earth, and a heavier equatorial leak

138
00:08:33,895 --> 00:08:36,160
Cassini flew through on its last dives.

139
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:41,160
The mass is too small for a forever-disk,

140
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:45,089
so the clock sits somewhere between about a hundred and

141
00:08:45,089 --> 00:08:46,660
three hundred million years.

142
00:08:46,660 --> 00:08:51,660
The rings are a phase. Saturn is the thing that remains.

143
00:08:51,660 --> 00:08:54,993
What else in the sky are you treating as permanent

144
00:08:54,993 --> 00:08:56,660
only because it is bright?

145
00:08:56,660 --> 00:08:59,013
The rings are the clearest case.

146
00:08:59,013 --> 00:09:02,933
The mystery is how much of that sky is temporary.

147
00:09:02,933 --> 00:09:06,070
Next: What Happens When the Last Star Dies?
